{"experiments":[  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"In successful edits (train.parquet), delete operations yield a significantly more negative average MW_delta than insert or replace operations, reflecting that deletions reduce molecular weight more drastically.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare the distribution of MW_delta across action_type categories (delete, insert, replace) in successful edits to test whether deletions produce larger weight reductions.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and drop any rows with null MW_delta or action_type.\n2. Group the data by action_type and compute the mean and median MW_delta for each category.\n3. Use a one-way ANOVA or Kruskal–Wallis test to assess whether MW_delta differs across the three action_type groups.\n4. If the global test is significant, perform post-hoc pairwise comparisons (e.g., Tukey’s HSD or Dunn’s test) to identify which groups differ.\n5. Summarize the results in a table and visualize boxplots of MW_delta by action_type.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Table of mean and median MW_delta per action_type.\n2. ANOVA/Kruskal–Wallis test statistic and p-value.\n3. Post-hoc pairwise comparison results.\n4. Boxplot of MW_delta by action_type."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Unsuccessful edits (train_neg.parquet) originate from shorter and less detailed prompts than successful edits, so the median prompt length is lower in train_neg than in train.parquet.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test whether the textual prompt length differs between successful and unsuccessful edits.",  
      "steps":"1. Load both train.parquet and train_neg.parquet.\n2. Drop any rows with null prompt.\n3. For each dataset, compute a new column prompt_length as the number of characters (or tokens) in the prompt string.\n4. Compare the distributions of prompt_length between the two datasets using a two-sample t-test or Mann–Whitney U test.\n5. Plot density or boxplot overlays of prompt_length for the two groups.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Summary statistics (mean, median, IQR) of prompt_length for train.parquet vs train_neg.parquet.\n2. Test statistic and p-value for the distribution comparison.\n3. Density plot or boxplot overlay of prompt_length distributions."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"For the water-solubility optimization task (task_id = 101) in successful edits, retaining the Murcko scaffold leads to smaller target_delta improvements compared to edits that change the scaffold, indicating scaffold changes drive larger solubility gains.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Within task_id 101, assess whether edits that break (non-retain) the Murcko scaffold produce larger target_delta than those that retain it.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and filter rows to task_id == 101, dropping null target_delta or murcko_scaffold_retained.\n2. Split examples into two groups by murcko_scaffold_retained (true vs false).\n3. Compute mean and median target_delta for each group.\n4. Use a two-sample t-test or Mann–Whitney U test to compare target_delta between scaffold-retained and scaffold-broken edits.\n5. Plot boxplots or violin plots of target_delta by scaffold retention status.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Mean and median target_delta for scaffold-retained vs scaffold-broken groups under task 101.\n2. Test statistic and p-value for the group comparison.\n3. Boxplot/violin plot visualizing the difference."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"In successful edits, increases in the number of rings (ring_count_delta > 0) correlate with higher synthetic accessibility penalties (SA_delta), because more rings make synthesis harder.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compute ring count changes between input and output molecules and test their correlation with SA_delta.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and drop rows with null SA_delta.\n2. Use RDKit to parse input_smiles and output_smiles, compute their number of rings (e.g., rdMolDescriptors.CalcNumRings).\n3. Derive ring_count_delta = output_ring_count − input_ring_count.\n4. Compute Pearson or Spearman correlation between ring_count_delta and SA_delta.\n5. Fit a linear regression model SA_delta ~ ring_count_delta + C(task_id) to estimate the effect while controlling for task.\n6. Plot ring_count_delta vs SA_delta with a trendline.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Correlation coefficient (and p-value) between ring_count_delta and SA_delta.\n2. Regression summary for SA_delta ~ ring_count_delta + task fixed effects.\n3. Scatter plot of ring_count_delta vs SA_delta with fitted line."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"The ratio of MW_delta to target_delta (|MW_delta/target_delta|) is higher on average in unsuccessful edits than in successful edits, indicating inefficient weight changes when the optimization fails.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare the distribution of the absolute MW_delta/target_delta ratio between train.parquet and train_neg.parquet.",  
      "steps":"1. Load both datasets and drop rows where target_delta is zero or null, and MW_delta is null.\n2. Compute abs_ratio = abs(MW_delta / target_delta) for each row.\n3. Compare the distributions of abs_ratio between successful and unsuccessful datasets using a Mann–Whitney U test.\n4. Summarize median and IQR of abs_ratio for each dataset.\n5. Plot boxplots or violin plots of abs_ratio by dataset type.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Median and IQR of abs_ratio in both datasets.\n2. U-test statistic and p-value.\n3. Boxplot/violin plot comparing abs_ratio distributions."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"The correlation between SA_delta and QED_delta varies by action_type in successful edits: insert operations show a weaker negative correlation than delete or replace.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Within train.parquet, compute and compare the SA_delta–QED_delta correlation across action_type categories.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and drop rows with null SA_delta or QED_delta or action_type.\n2. For each action_type (insert, delete, replace), compute Pearson correlation and p-value between SA_delta and QED_delta.\n3. Use Fisher’s z-test to assess whether correlations differ significantly between pairs of action_types.\n4. Create a table of correlations with confidence intervals.\n5. Plot scatter plots with separate trendlines for each action_type.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Pearson r and p-value for each action_type.\n2. Fisher’s z-test results comparing correlations between action_types.\n3. Combined scatter plot with colored trendlines."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Some optimization tasks exhibit more variability in outcomes: tasks targeting permeability (task_id = 105) have higher variance in target_delta than tasks targeting drug-likeness (103).",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Quantify and compare the variance of target_delta across different task_ids, focusing on 105 vs 103.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and drop rows with null target_delta.\n2. Group data by task_id and compute variance and standard deviation of target_delta for each task.\n3. Use Levene’s test or Bartlett’s test to compare variances between task 105 and task 103.\n4. Present a ranked list of task_ids by target_delta variance.\n5. Visualize variances via a bar chart.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Table of target_delta variance and SD for each task_id.\n2. Variance comparison test statistic and p-value for 105 vs 103.\n3. Bar chart of variances by task_id."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Within successful edits, high QED improvements (QED_delta > 0.2) are associated with a lower rate of scaffold retention than modest or negative QED_delta, indicating that large drug-likeness gains often require scaffold changes.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test whether the proportion of murcko_scaffold_retained differs between high QED_delta and low QED_delta groups.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and drop rows with null QED_delta or murcko_scaffold_retained.\n2. Define two groups: high_QED = QED_delta > 0.2, low_QED = QED_delta ≤ 0.2.\n3. Compute the proportion of murcko_scaffold_retained (true) in each group.\n4. Use a chi-square test of independence to assess whether scaffold retention is associated with the QED_delta group.\n5. Report counts, proportions, test statistic, and p-value. Plot a clustered bar chart.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Counts and retention rates for high_QED vs low_QED groups.\n2. Chi-square test statistic and p-value.\n3. Clustered bar chart of scaffold retention by QED group."  
    }  
  }  
]}
